\title{Committee assignment}
\date{2026-11-25T15:10:33Z}
\tag{algorithms}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\p{Consider the following scenario:

\ul{
  \li{There are a number of committees; each committee has a desired number of people.}
  \li{There is a set of people; each person has (1) a list of committees they are willing to be on, and (2) a maximum total number of committees they are willing to be on.}
}

Given these constraints, the goal is to assign people to committees so
that (1) everyone's preferences are respected, and (2) each committee
is full with the required number of people, if possible.
}

\p{We can solve this by creating a flow network, as follows:

\ul{
  \li{There is a vertex for each person, a vertex for each committee, and special source and sink vertices #{s} and #{t}.}
  \li{We add an edge from #{s} each each person, with capacity equal to the total number of committees that person is willing to be on.}
  \li{From each person, we add edges of capacity #{1} to each committee they are willing to be on.}
  \li{From each committee, we add an edge to the sink #{t} with capacity equal to the desired size of the committee.}
}

Any valid flow on this network corresponds to a valid committee assignment that respects everyone's preferences; a unit of flow represents an assignment of one person to one committee.  Each person can only be on committees they prefer, since those are the only edges we added; each person cannot be on more total committees than they want, since the flow through their vertex is limited by the edge from #{s}; and each committee can have at most the required number of people.  (Note it is important that the edges from people to committees have capacity #{1}, since otherwise it would be possible to have one person "serve multiple times" on the same committee, and this is probably not desired.)
}

\p{Since valid flows correspond to valid assignments, it is possible to fill all the committees if and only if a maximum flow saturates all the edges going into #{t}.
}

